<Layout title="Coding Quiz for Kids – Find the Bug Debugging Game" description="A free coding quiz for kids: predict what code will do, spot the bug, and learn how programmers debug. Eight puzzles with explanations, no timer, no sign-up."><div class="wrap"><div class="page-head"><span class="eyebrow">The bug detective club</span><h1>Every bug<br/>leaves a clue.<span class="h1-topic">A debugging quiz for kids</span></h1><p>Predict the output, spot a little mistake, and discover how programmers think. No timer. No pressure. Just eight chances for an “aha!”</p></div><div class="panel" style="max-width:780px;margin-bottom:60px"><div class="quiz-progress" aria-hidden="true"><span id="quiz-bar"></span></div><p class="eyebrow" id="quiz-position"></p><h2 id="quiz-question" tabindex="-1"></h2><pre id="quiz-code" class="note" hidden></pre><div id="quiz-options" class="quiz-options"></div><div class="feedback" id="quiz-feedback" role="status">Pick the answer you think fits.</div><div class="actions"><button class="button dark" id="quiz-next" hidden>Next clue →</button><button class="button" id="quiz-restart" hidden>Play again ↻</button><a href="/python/" id="quiz-learn" hidden>Explore the Python trail →</a></div><noscript><p>Turn on JavaScript to try the detective quiz.</p></noscript></div><section class="section" style="padding-top:0"><span class="eyebrow">How programmers debug</span><h2>Four steps to catch a bug</h2><p>A bug is a mistake that makes a program do something unexpected. Every coder meets bugs, every day. Here’s the detective routine this quiz practices.</p><div class="grid2"><div class="card"><h3>1. Read the clue</h3><p>Look at what the program actually did. An error message’s last line often names the problem and the line where it happened.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>2. Predict</h3><p>Read the code one line at a time, from top to bottom. What should each line do? Where does your prediction stop matching what happened?</p></div><div class="card"><h3>3. Change one thing</h3><p>Fix the smallest suspicious piece first: a missing quote, a wrong number, the wrong order, or an indent in the wrong place.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>4. Test again</h3><p>Run the program again. Try a different input too. If something new goes wrong, that’s just your next clue.</p></div></div><div class="note"><strong>Practice on real code:</strong> fix a <a href="/blocks/debugging/">buggy block program</a>, or open the <a href="/playground/">Python playground</a> and follow the red message.</div></section></div><script>import '../scripts/quiz';</script></Layout>
